annihilator -- the annihilator ideal
************************************

  * Usage: 
        ann M
        annihilator M
  * Inputs:
      * M, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, a *note module:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, or a *note ring element:
        (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,,
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Strategy => a *note symbol: (Macaulay2Doc)Symbol,, default value null,
        either Quotient or Intersection
  * Outputs:
      * an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, the annihilator ideal
        $\mathrm{ann}(M) = \{ f \in R | fM = 0 \}$ where $R$ is the ring of $M$

Description
===========

You may use ann as a synonym for annihilator.

As an example, we compute the annihilator of the canonical module of the
rational quartic curve.

+--------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[a..d];                                            |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : J = monomialCurveIdeal(R,{1,3,4})                        |
|                                                              |
|                        3      2     2    2    3    2         |
|o2 = ideal (b*c - a*d, c  - b*d , a*c  - b d, b  - a c)       |
|                                                              |
|o2 : Ideal of R                                               |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : M = Ext^2(R^1/J, R)                                      |
|                                                              |
|o3 = subquotient ({-4} | c b 0  a  0 0 |, {-4} | -b2 c a  0 |)|
|                  {-4} | d 0 b  0  a 0 |  {-4} | -ac d b  0 | |
|                  {-4} | 0 d -c 0  0 a |  {-4} | -bd 0 -c a | |
|                  {-4} | 0 0 0  -d c b |  {-4} | -c2 0 -d b | |
|                                                              |
|                               4                              |
|o3 : R-module, subquotient of R                               |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : annihilator M                                            |
|                                                              |
|                        3      2     2    2    3    2         |
|o4 = ideal (b*c - a*d, c  - b*d , a*c  - b d, b  - a c)       |
|                                                              |
|o4 : Ideal of R                                               |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+

For another example, we compute the annihilator of an element in a quotient
ring.

+--------------------------+
|i5 : A = R/(a*b, a*c, a*d)|
|                          |
|o5 = A                    |
|                          |
|o5 : QuotientRing         |
+--------------------------+
|i6 : ann a                |
|                          |
|o6 = ideal (d, c, b)      |
|                          |
|o6 : Ideal of A           |
+--------------------------+

Currently two algorithms to compute annihilators are implemented. The default
is to compute the annihilator of each generator of the module M and to
intersect these two by two. Each annihilator is done using a submodule
quotient. The other algorithm computes the annihilator in one large computation
and is used if Strategy => Quotient is specified.

+-------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : annihilator(M, Strategy => Quotient)              |
|                                                       |
|                        3      2     2    2    3    2  |
|o7 = ideal (b*c - a*d, c  - b*d , a*c  - b d, b  - a c)|
|                                                       |
|o7 : Ideal of R                                        |
+-------------------------------------------------------+

