saturate -- saturation of ideal or submodule
********************************************

  * Usage: 
        saturate(I, J)
        saturate I
  * Inputs:
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, a *note monomial ideal:
        (Macaulay2Doc)MonomialIdeal,, or a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,,
      * J, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, a *note monomial ideal:
        (Macaulay2Doc)MonomialIdeal,, a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, or
        a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, if not present, then
        the ideal generated by the variables of the ring is used
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Strategy: saturate_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmStrategy=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => a
        *note thing: (Macaulay2Doc)Thing,, default value null, specifies the
        algorithm
      * MinimalGenerators => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,,
        default value true, indicates whether the output should be *note
        trimmed: (Macaulay2Doc)trim,
      * BasisElementLimit => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value
        infinity, passed onto *note gb(...,BasisElementLimit=>...):
        (Macaulay2Doc)gb,;
      * DegreeLimit => a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, default value {},
        passed onto *note gb(...,DegreeLimit=>...): (Macaulay2Doc)gb,;
      * PairLimit => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value
        infinity, passed onto *note gb(...,PairLimit=>...): (Macaulay2Doc)gb,;
  * Outputs:
      * an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal, or a *note module:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, the saturation $I:J^\infty = \{f | f J^N\subset
        I \text{ for some } N>0\}$

Description
===========

If $I$ is either an ideal or a submodule of a module $M$, the saturation $I :
J^\infty$ is defined to be the set of elements $f$ in the ring or $M$ such that
$f J^N$ is contained in $I$, for some $N$ large enough.

For example, one way to homogenize an ideal is to homogenize the generators and
then saturate with respect to the homogenizing variable.

+-------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/32003[a..d];                               |
+-------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : I = ideal(a^3-b, a^4-c)                           |
|                                                       |
|             3       4                                 |
|o2 = ideal (a  - b, a  - c)                            |
|                                                       |
|o2 : Ideal of R                                        |
+-------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : Ih = homogenize(I,d)                              |
|                                                       |
|                        2     2    3      2   3      2 |
|o3 = ideal (a*b - c*d, a c - b d, b  - a*c , a  - b*d )|
|                                                       |
|o3 : Ideal of R                                        |
+-------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : saturate(Ih,d)                                    |
|                                                       |
|                        2     2    3      2   3      2 |
|o4 = ideal (a*b - c*d, a c - b d, b  - a*c , a  - b*d )|
|                                                       |
|o4 : Ideal of R                                        |
+-------------------------------------------------------+

We can use this command to remove graded submodules of finite length.

+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : m = ideal vars R                                     |
|                                                          |
|o5 = ideal (a, b, c, d)                                   |
|                                                          |
|o5 : Ideal of R                                           |
+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : M = R^1 / (a * m^2)                                  |
|                                                          |
|o6 = cokernel | a3 a2b a2c a2d ab2 abc abd ac2 acd ad2 |  |
|                                                          |
|                            1                             |
|o6 : R-module, quotient of R                              |
+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : M / saturate 0_M                                     |
|                                                          |
|o7 = cokernel | a a3 a2b a2c a2d ab2 abc abd ac2 acd ad2 ||
|                                                          |
|                            1                             |
|o7 : R-module, quotient of R                              |
+----------------------------------------------------------+

If $I$ and $J$ are both monomial ideals, then a faster algorithm is used.
Otherwise, when needed, Gröbner bases will be computed, and if the computation
succeeds the result is cached inside the first argument. Partial computations
are not yet cached, but this may change in a future version.

